window.PROCESS_TASKS["07"] = {"number":"07","title":"Manufacturing Glass Bottles","icon":"🫙","image":"glass-bottles.svg","type":"Linear","stages":7,"category":"LinearProcess","description":"The diagram illustrates the main stages in the manufacture of glass bottles from raw materials.","sourceUrl":"https://www.bvglas.de/en/about-glass/glass-is-a-multitalented-material","sourceName":"German Glass Industry Association","prompt":"The diagram illustrates the main stages in the manufacture of glass bottles from raw materials.","topicWords":["glass","bottles","glass bottles"],"stageModels":[{"label":"Preparing materials","exact":["sand","soda ash","limestone","cullet","preparing materials"],"roots":["sand","soda","limestone"],"ref":"Silica sand, soda ash, limestone and recycled glass (cullet) are selected."},{"label":"Batch mixing","exact":["mixed","weighed","batch","blended","batch mixing"],"roots":["mixed","weighed","batch"],"ref":"The ingredients are weighed and blended into a uniform batch."},{"label":"Melting","exact":["furnace","melted","molten","melting"],"roots":["furnace","melted","molten"],"ref":"The batch is heated in a furnace until molten glass is produced."},{"label":"Gob formation","exact":["gobs","portions","measured","gob formation"],"roots":["gobs","portions","measured"],"ref":"The molten glass is divided into measured portions called gobs."},{"label":"Moulding","exact":["mould","mold","shaped","bottles","moulding"],"roots":["mould","mold","shaped"],"ref":"The gobs are shaped into bottles in a forming machine."},{"label":"Annealing","exact":["annealed","annealing","cooled"],"roots":["annealed","annealing","cooled"],"ref":"The newly formed bottles cool gradually in an annealing lehr."},{"label":"Inspection and packing","exact":["inspected","inspection","packed","delivery","inspection and packing"],"roots":["inspected","inspection","packed"],"ref":"The finished bottles are checked before being packed for delivery."}],"analysis":{"type":["linear"],"stages":["7"],"first":["sand","soda ash","limestone","cullet"],"last":["inspected","inspection","packed","delivery"],"features":["sand","soda ash","limestone","cullet","mixed","weighed","batch","blended","furnace","melted","molten","gobs","portions","measured","mould","mold","shaped","bottles","annealed","annealing","cooled","inspected","inspection","packed","delivery"],"body1":["sand","soda ash","limestone","cullet","mixed","weighed","batch","blended","furnace","melted","molten","gobs","portions","measured"],"body2":["mould","mold","shaped","bottles","annealed","annealing","cooled","inspected","inspection","packed","delivery"]},"intro":{"reference":"The diagram illustrates the main stages in the manufacture of glass bottles.","concepts":[["diagram","figure","process"],["illustrates","shows","depicts"],["glass","bottles","glass bottles"],["stages","cycle","production","manufacture","illustrated"]],"minWords":8},"overview":{"reference":"Overall, glass-bottle manufacturing is a linear seven-stage process, beginning with the selection of mineral ingredients and recycled glass and ending with quality inspection and packaging. The materials are first melted and shaped before the products are slowly cooled.","concepts":[["overall","in general"],["sand","soda ash","limestone","cullet","inspected","inspection","packed","delivery"],["stages","cycle","sequence","process"],["glass","bottles","glass bottles"]],"minWords":35},"body1":{"reference":"At the beginning, silica sand, soda ash, limestone and pieces of recycled glass, known as cullet, are collected. These ingredients are weighed and blended into a uniform batch. The prepared mixture is then fed into a furnace and heated until it becomes molten glass. Subsequently, the hot liquid is separated into measured portions, called gobs, for the forming stage.","concepts":[["sand","soda ash","limestone","cullet"],["mixed","weighed","batch","blended"],["furnace","melted","molten"],["gobs","portions","measured"]],"minWords":55,"sequence":["initially","at the beginning","first","then","after","next","once","subsequently"]},"body2":{"reference":"The measured gobs enter a moulding machine, where each portion is shaped into a glass bottle. Once formed, the bottles pass through an annealing lehr and are cooled gradually to reduce internal stress. Finally, the finished bottles undergo quality inspection, after which those that meet the required standard are packed and made ready for delivery.","concepts":[["mould","mold","shaped","bottles"],["annealed","annealing","cooled"],["inspected","inspection","packed","delivery"]],"minWords":55,"sequence":["finally","then","subsequently","after","once","eventually","next"]},"fullReference":"The diagram illustrates the main stages in the manufacture of glass bottles.\n\nOverall, glass-bottle manufacturing is a linear seven-stage process, beginning with the selection of mineral ingredients and recycled glass and ending with quality inspection and packaging. The materials are first melted and shaped before the products are slowly cooled.\n\nAt the beginning, silica sand, soda ash, limestone and pieces of recycled glass, known as cullet, are collected. These ingredients are weighed and blended into a uniform batch. The prepared mixture is then fed into a furnace and heated until it becomes molten glass. Subsequently, the hot liquid is separated into measured portions, called gobs, for the forming stage.\n\nThe measured gobs enter a moulding machine, where each portion is shaped into a glass bottle. Once formed, the bottles pass through an annealing lehr and are cooled gradually to reduce internal stress. Finally, the finished bottles undergo quality inspection, after which those that meet the required standard are packed and made ready for delivery."};
window.PROCESS_TASKS["08"] = {"number":"08","title":"Producing Olive Oil","icon":"🫒","image":"olive-oil.svg","type":"Linear","stages":8,"category":"LinearProcess","description":"The diagram shows a typical linear process used to produce filtered olive oil.","sourceUrl":"https://ucfoodquality.ucdavis.edu/olive-oil/olive-oil-processing","sourceName":"UC Davis Food Quality","prompt":"The diagram shows a typical linear process used to produce filtered olive oil.","topicWords":["olive","oil","olive oil"],"stageModels":[{"label":"Harvesting","exact":["harvest","picked","olives","harvesting"],"roots":["harvest","picked","olives"],"ref":"Ripe olives are picked from trees and transported to a mill."},{"label":"Cleaning","exact":["leaves","washed","cleaned","cleaning"],"roots":["leaves","washed","cleaned"],"ref":"Leaves are removed and the olives are washed."},{"label":"Crushing","exact":["crushed","paste","milled","crushing"],"roots":["crushed","paste","milled"],"ref":"The cleaned fruit is crushed into olive paste."},{"label":"Malaxation","exact":["malaxation","slowly mixed","stirred"],"roots":["malaxation","slowly","stirred"],"ref":"The paste is mixed slowly to help oil droplets combine."},{"label":"Centrifugation","exact":["centrifuge","separated","solids","centrifugation"],"roots":["centrifuge","separated","solids"],"ref":"A centrifuge separates the oil from water and solids."},{"label":"Clarification","exact":["filtered","filtration","clarified","clarification"],"roots":["filtered","filtration","clarified"],"ref":"The oil is clarified, for example through filtration."},{"label":"Storage","exact":["storage","tanks","stored"],"roots":["storage","tanks","stored"],"ref":"The finished oil is held in suitable storage tanks."},{"label":"Bottling","exact":["bottled","bottles","packaged","bottling"],"roots":["bottled","bottles","packaged"],"ref":"The oil is filled into bottles and prepared for sale."}],"analysis":{"type":["linear"],"stages":["8"],"first":["harvest","picked","olives"],"last":["bottled","bottles","packaged"],"features":["harvest","picked","olives","leaves","washed","cleaned","crushed","paste","milled","malaxation","slowly mixed","stirred","centrifuge","separated","solids","filtered","filtration","clarified","storage","tanks","stored","bottled","bottles","packaged"],"body1":["harvest","picked","olives","leaves","washed","cleaned","crushed","paste","milled","malaxation","slowly mixed","stirred"],"body2":["centrifuge","separated","solids","filtered","filtration","clarified","storage","tanks","stored","bottled","bottles","packaged"]},"intro":{"reference":"The diagram illustrates the process used to produce filtered olive oil.","concepts":[["diagram","figure","process"],["illustrates","shows","depicts"],["olive","oil","olive oil"],["stages","cycle","production","manufacture","illustrated"]],"minWords":8},"overview":{"reference":"Overall, the illustrated production route has eight linear stages, starting with olive harvesting and finishing with bottling. After the olives are cleaned and crushed, their paste is mixed and mechanically separated before the oil is clarified and stored.","concepts":[["overall","in general"],["harvest","picked","olives","bottled","bottles","packaged"],["stages","cycle","sequence","process"],["olive","oil","olive oil"]],"minWords":35},"body1":{"reference":"Initially, ripe olives are harvested from trees and transported to an oil mill. Leaves and unwanted material are removed, and the fruit is washed thoroughly. The clean olives are then crushed into a thick paste. During malaxation, this paste is stirred slowly so that small droplets of oil can combine, preparing the mixture for extraction.","concepts":[["harvest","picked","olives"],["leaves","washed","cleaned"],["crushed","paste","milled"],["malaxation","slowly mixed","stirred"]],"minWords":55,"sequence":["initially","at the beginning","first","then","after","next","once","subsequently"]},"body2":{"reference":"Next, the olive paste passes through a centrifuge, which separates the oil from water and solid material. In the illustrated route, the resulting oil is clarified through filtration to remove remaining suspended particles. It is then transferred to suitable storage tanks. In the final stage, the finished olive oil is poured into bottles and prepared for distribution.","concepts":[["centrifuge","separated","solids"],["filtered","filtration","clarified"],["storage","tanks","stored"],["bottled","bottles","packaged"]],"minWords":55,"sequence":["finally","then","subsequently","after","once","eventually","next"]},"fullReference":"The diagram illustrates the process used to produce filtered olive oil.\n\nOverall, the illustrated production route has eight linear stages, starting with olive harvesting and finishing with bottling. After the olives are cleaned and crushed, their paste is mixed and mechanically separated before the oil is clarified and stored.\n\nInitially, ripe olives are harvested from trees and transported to an oil mill. Leaves and unwanted material are removed, and the fruit is washed thoroughly. The clean olives are then crushed into a thick paste. During malaxation, this paste is stirred slowly so that small droplets of oil can combine, preparing the mixture for extraction.\n\nNext, the olive paste passes through a centrifuge, which separates the oil from water and solid material. In the illustrated route, the resulting oil is clarified through filtration to remove remaining suspended particles. It is then transferred to suitable storage tanks. In the final stage, the finished olive oil is poured into bottles and prepared for distribution."};
window.PROCESS_TASKS["09"] = {"number":"09","title":"The Water Cycle","icon":"💧","image":"water-cycle.svg","type":"Cyclical","stages":6,"category":"CyclicalProcess","description":"The diagram illustrates a simplified natural water cycle between the surface of Earth and the atmosphere.","sourceUrl":"https://www.usgs.gov/special-topics/water-science-school/water-cycle","sourceName":"U.S. Geological Survey","prompt":"The diagram illustrates a simplified natural water cycle between the surface of Earth and the atmosphere.","topicWords":["water","rain","water cycle"],"stageModels":[{"label":"Evaporation","exact":["evaporates","evaporation","sun"],"roots":["evaporates","evaporation"],"ref":"Heat from the Sun causes surface water to evaporate."},{"label":"Condensation","exact":["condenses","condensation","clouds"],"roots":["condenses","condensation","clouds"],"ref":"The rising water vapour cools and condenses into clouds."},{"label":"Precipitation","exact":["precipitation","rain","snow"],"roots":["precipitation","rain","snow"],"ref":"Cloud droplets fall as rain or snow."},{"label":"Surface runoff","exact":["runoff","streams","rivers","surface runoff"],"roots":["runoff","streams","rivers"],"ref":"Some precipitation flows over the land into streams and rivers."},{"label":"Infiltration","exact":["infiltration","groundwater","soil"],"roots":["infiltration","groundwater","soil"],"ref":"Another portion seeps into the ground as groundwater."},{"label":"Collection","exact":["collection","oceans","returns"],"roots":["collection","oceans","returns"],"ref":"Surface and groundwater flow back to lakes and oceans."}],"analysis":{"type":["cyclical"],"stages":["6"],"first":["evaporates","evaporation","sun"],"last":["collection","oceans","returns"],"features":["evaporates","evaporation","sun","condenses","condensation","clouds","precipitation","rain","snow","runoff","streams","rivers","infiltration","groundwater","soil","collection","oceans","returns"],"body1":["evaporates","evaporation","sun","condenses","condensation","clouds","precipitation","rain","snow"],"body2":["runoff","streams","rivers","infiltration","groundwater","soil","collection","oceans","returns"]},"intro":{"reference":"The diagram illustrates the natural water cycle between the Earth's surface and the atmosphere.","concepts":[["diagram","figure","process"],["illustrates","shows","depicts"],["water","rain","water cycle"],["stages","cycle","production","manufacture","illustrated"]],"minWords":8},"overview":{"reference":"Overall, the water cycle is a continuous six-stage system driven by solar energy. Water evaporates into the atmosphere, returns to the surface as precipitation and eventually collects again in water bodies through runoff and underground movement.","concepts":[["overall","in general"],["evaporates","evaporation","sun","collection","oceans","returns"],["stages","cycle","sequence","process"],["water","rain","water cycle"]],"minWords":35},"body1":{"reference":"The cycle begins when energy from the Sun heats water in oceans, lakes and other surface reservoirs, causing evaporation. As the resulting water vapour rises, it cools and condenses into tiny droplets that form clouds. When these droplets become sufficiently large, they fall back to the ground in the form of rain or snow, a stage known as precipitation.","concepts":[["evaporates","evaporation","sun"],["condenses","condensation","clouds"],["precipitation","rain","snow"]],"minWords":55,"sequence":["initially","at the beginning","first","then","after","next","once","subsequently"]},"body2":{"reference":"After reaching the Earth's surface, the water follows two main routes. Some travels over the land as surface runoff, entering streams and rivers. The rest infiltrates the soil and contributes to underground water reserves. Both surface water and groundwater can ultimately return to lakes or oceans, where continued heating causes evaporation again and the cycle repeats.","concepts":[["runoff","streams","rivers"],["infiltration","groundwater","soil"],["collection","oceans","returns"]],"minWords":55,"sequence":["finally","then","subsequently","after","once","eventually","next"]},"fullReference":"The diagram illustrates the natural water cycle between the Earth's surface and the atmosphere.\n\nOverall, the water cycle is a continuous six-stage system driven by solar energy. Water evaporates into the atmosphere, returns to the surface as precipitation and eventually collects again in water bodies through runoff and underground movement.\n\nThe cycle begins when energy from the Sun heats water in oceans, lakes and other surface reservoirs, causing evaporation. As the resulting water vapour rises, it cools and condenses into tiny droplets that form clouds. When these droplets become sufficiently large, they fall back to the ground in the form of rain or snow, a stage known as precipitation.\n\nAfter reaching the Earth's surface, the water follows two main routes. Some travels over the land as surface runoff, entering streams and rivers. The rest infiltrates the soil and contributes to underground water reserves. Both surface water and groundwater can ultimately return to lakes or oceans, where continued heating causes evaporation again and the cycle repeats."};
window.PROCESS_TASKS["10"] = {"number":"10","title":"The Carbon Cycle","icon":"🌿","image":"carbon-cycle.svg","type":"Cyclical","stages":7,"category":"CyclicalProcess","description":"The diagram presents a simplified biological carbon cycle between the atmosphere, plants, animals and decomposers.","sourceUrl":"https://www.noaa.gov/education/resource-collections/climate/carbon-cycle","sourceName":"NOAA","prompt":"The diagram presents a simplified biological carbon cycle between the atmosphere, plants, animals and decomposers.","topicWords":["carbon","carbon dioxide","co2"],"stageModels":[{"label":"Atmospheric CO₂","exact":["atmosphere","carbon dioxide","co2","atmospheric co₂"],"roots":["atmosphere","carbon"],"ref":"Carbon dioxide is present in the atmosphere."},{"label":"Photosynthesis","exact":["photosynthesis","absorbed","plants"],"roots":["photosynthesis","absorbed","plants"],"ref":"Plants absorb carbon dioxide during photosynthesis."},{"label":"Plant biomass","exact":["biomass","plant tissues","stored","plant biomass"],"roots":["biomass","plant","stored"],"ref":"Carbon is stored in growing plant tissues."},{"label":"Consumption","exact":["animals","consume","feeding","consumption"],"roots":["animals","consume","feeding"],"ref":"Animals obtain carbon by feeding on plants."},{"label":"Organic remains","exact":["dead","remains","waste","organic remains"],"roots":["dead","remains","waste"],"ref":"Dead organisms and waste enter the soil."},{"label":"Decomposition","exact":["decomposition","decomposers","microbes"],"roots":["decomposition","decomposers","microbes"],"ref":"Decomposers break down the organic material."},{"label":"Return to air","exact":["respiration","released","atmosphere","return to air"],"roots":["respiration","released","atmosphere"],"ref":"Respiration by living things and decomposers releases CO₂."}],"analysis":{"type":["cyclical"],"stages":["7"],"first":["atmosphere","carbon dioxide","co2"],"last":["respiration","released","atmosphere"],"features":["atmosphere","carbon dioxide","co2","photosynthesis","absorbed","plants","biomass","plant tissues","stored","animals","consume","feeding","dead","remains","waste","decomposition","decomposers","microbes","respiration","released"],"body1":["atmosphere","carbon dioxide","co2","photosynthesis","absorbed","plants","biomass","plant tissues","stored","animals","consume","feeding"],"body2":["dead","remains","waste","decomposition","decomposers","microbes","respiration","released","atmosphere"]},"intro":{"reference":"The diagram illustrates a simplified biological carbon cycle.","concepts":[["diagram","figure","process"],["illustrates","shows","depicts"],["carbon","carbon dioxide","co2"],["stages","cycle","production","manufacture","illustrated"]],"minWords":8},"overview":{"reference":"Overall, this simplified seven-stage carbon cycle transfers carbon from the air into living organisms and eventually returns it to the atmosphere. Photosynthesis takes up carbon dioxide, whereas respiration and decomposition release carbon again.","concepts":[["overall","in general"],["atmosphere","carbon dioxide","co2","respiration","released","atmosphere"],["stages","cycle","sequence","process"],["carbon","carbon dioxide","co2"]],"minWords":35},"body1":{"reference":"At the start, carbon dioxide is present in the atmosphere. Green plants absorb this gas through photosynthesis and use it to build new tissues as they grow. The resulting carbon becomes part of plant biomass. When animals eat plants, some of the stored carbon is transferred into animal bodies, moving through the food chain.","concepts":[["atmosphere","carbon dioxide","co2"],["photosynthesis","absorbed","plants"],["biomass","plant tissues","stored"],["animals","consume","feeding"]],"minWords":55,"sequence":["initially","at the beginning","first","then","after","next","once","subsequently"]},"body2":{"reference":"When plants and animals die, their remains, together with organic waste, enter the soil. Microorganisms and other decomposers gradually break down this material. Throughout the cycle, organisms also respire, releasing carbon dioxide back into the air. This atmospheric carbon is then available for plants to absorb again, allowing the biological cycle to continue. In this way, the atmosphere remains connected to land ecosystems through repeated carbon exchange.","concepts":[["dead","remains","waste"],["decomposition","decomposers","microbes"],["respiration","released","atmosphere"]],"minWords":55,"sequence":["finally","then","subsequently","after","once","eventually","next"]},"fullReference":"The diagram illustrates a simplified biological carbon cycle.\n\nOverall, this simplified seven-stage carbon cycle transfers carbon from the air into living organisms and eventually returns it to the atmosphere. Photosynthesis takes up carbon dioxide, whereas respiration and decomposition release carbon again.\n\nAt the start, carbon dioxide is present in the atmosphere. Green plants absorb this gas through photosynthesis and use it to build new tissues as they grow. The resulting carbon becomes part of plant biomass. When animals eat plants, some of the stored carbon is transferred into animal bodies, moving through the food chain.\n\nWhen plants and animals die, their remains, together with organic waste, enter the soil. Microorganisms and other decomposers gradually break down this material. Throughout the cycle, organisms also respire, releasing carbon dioxide back into the air. This atmospheric carbon is then available for plants to absorb again, allowing the biological cycle to continue. In this way, the atmosphere remains connected to land ecosystems through repeated carbon exchange."};
window.PROCESS_TASKS["11"] = {"number":"11","title":"The Nitrogen Cycle","icon":"🧪","image":"nitrogen-cycle.svg","type":"Cyclical","stages":8,"category":"CyclicalProcess","description":"The diagram shows the main transformations in a simplified nitrogen cycle, including a return route to the atmosphere.","sourceUrl":"https://gml.noaa.gov/outreach/info_activities/pdfs/CTA_nitrogen_cycle.pdf","sourceName":"NOAA Global Monitoring Laboratory","prompt":"The diagram shows the main transformations in a simplified nitrogen cycle, including a return route to the atmosphere.","topicWords":["nitrogen","nitrates","ammonium","n2"],"stageModels":[{"label":"Atmospheric nitrogen","exact":["nitrogen gas","atmosphere","n2","atmospheric nitrogen"],"roots":["nitrogen","atmosphere"],"ref":"Nitrogen gas (N₂) is present in the atmosphere."},{"label":"Nitrogen fixation","exact":["fixation","bacteria","ammonium","nitrogen fixation"],"roots":["fixation","bacteria","ammonium"],"ref":"Certain bacteria convert nitrogen gas into ammonium."},{"label":"Soil ammonium","exact":["ammonium","soil","nh4","soil ammonium"],"roots":["ammonium","soil"],"ref":"Ammonium becomes available in the soil."},{"label":"Nitrification","exact":["nitrification","nitrites","nitrates"],"roots":["nitrification","nitrites","nitrates"],"ref":"Bacteria turn ammonium into nitrites and then nitrates."},{"label":"Plant uptake","exact":["plants","uptake","assimilate","plant uptake"],"roots":["plants","uptake","assimilate"],"ref":"Plants absorb available nitrogen compounds from the soil."},{"label":"Organic nitrogen","exact":["animals","feeding","organic matter","organic nitrogen"],"roots":["animals","feeding","organic"],"ref":"Nitrogen enters animals and organic matter through feeding."},{"label":"Ammonification","exact":["ammonification","decomposers","ammonium"],"roots":["ammonification","decomposers","ammonium"],"ref":"Decomposers return organic nitrogen to soil ammonium."},{"label":"Denitrification","exact":["denitrification","nitrate","atmosphere"],"roots":["denitrification","nitrate","atmosphere"],"ref":"Other bacteria convert soil nitrate back into atmospheric N₂."}],"analysis":{"type":["cyclical"],"stages":["8"],"first":["nitrogen gas","atmosphere","n2"],"last":["denitrification","nitrate","atmosphere"],"features":["nitrogen gas","atmosphere","n2","fixation","bacteria","ammonium","soil","nh4","nitrification","nitrites","nitrates","plants","uptake","assimilate","animals","feeding","organic matter","ammonification","decomposers","denitrification","nitrate"],"body1":["nitrogen gas","atmosphere","n2","fixation","bacteria","ammonium","soil","nh4","nitrification","nitrites","nitrates"],"body2":["plants","uptake","assimilate","animals","feeding","organic matter","ammonification","decomposers","ammonium","denitrification","nitrate","atmosphere"]},"intro":{"reference":"The diagram illustrates the main stages and pathways in the nitrogen cycle.","concepts":[["diagram","figure","process"],["illustrates","shows","depicts"],["nitrogen","nitrates","ammonium","n2"],["stages","cycle","production","manufacture","illustrated"]],"minWords":8},"overview":{"reference":"Overall, nitrogen moves continuously between the atmosphere, soil and living organisms through a series of bacterial transformations. Fixation introduces usable nitrogen into the soil, while ammonification recycles it and denitrification returns nitrogen gas to the atmosphere.","concepts":[["overall","in general"],["nitrogen gas","atmosphere","n2","denitrification","nitrate","atmosphere"],["stages","cycle","sequence","process"],["nitrogen","nitrates","ammonium","n2"]],"minWords":35},"body1":{"reference":"The cycle begins with nitrogen gas in the atmosphere. Nitrogen-fixing bacteria convert some of this gas into ammonium, making nitrogen available within the soil. Other microorganisms carry out nitrification, changing ammonium first into nitrites and then into nitrates. These nitrogen compounds can subsequently be taken up through plant roots.","concepts":[["nitrogen gas","atmosphere","n2"],["fixation","bacteria","ammonium"],["ammonium","soil","nh4"],["nitrification","nitrites","nitrates"]],"minWords":55,"sequence":["initially","at the beginning","first","then","after","next","once","subsequently"]},"body2":{"reference":"Once plants have absorbed nitrogen, it can enter animal tissues through feeding. Dead plants, animal remains and waste provide organic material for decomposers, which release ammonium back into the soil by ammonification. A separate pathway completes the atmospheric loop: denitrifying bacteria convert some of the available nitrates into nitrogen gas, which returns to the air.","concepts":[["plants","uptake","assimilate"],["animals","feeding","organic matter"],["ammonification","decomposers","ammonium"],["denitrification","nitrate","atmosphere"]],"minWords":55,"sequence":["finally","then","subsequently","after","once","eventually","next"]},"fullReference":"The diagram illustrates the main stages and pathways in the nitrogen cycle.\n\nOverall, nitrogen moves continuously between the atmosphere, soil and living organisms through a series of bacterial transformations. Fixation introduces usable nitrogen into the soil, while ammonification recycles it and denitrification returns nitrogen gas to the atmosphere.\n\nThe cycle begins with nitrogen gas in the atmosphere. Nitrogen-fixing bacteria convert some of this gas into ammonium, making nitrogen available within the soil. Other microorganisms carry out nitrification, changing ammonium first into nitrites and then into nitrates. These nitrogen compounds can subsequently be taken up through plant roots.\n\nOnce plants have absorbed nitrogen, it can enter animal tissues through feeding. Dead plants, animal remains and waste provide organic material for decomposers, which release ammonium back into the soil by ammonification. A separate pathway completes the atmospheric loop: denitrifying bacteria convert some of the available nitrates into nitrogen gas, which returns to the air."};
window.PROCESS_TASKS["12"] = {"number":"12","title":"The Rock Cycle","icon":"🪨","image":"rock-cycle.svg","type":"Cyclical","stages":8,"category":"CyclicalProcess","description":"The diagram shows one simplified route through the rock cycle, linking igneous, sedimentary and metamorphic rocks.","sourceUrl":"https://www.geolsoc.org.uk/ks3/gsl/education/resources/rockcycle.html","sourceName":"Geological Society of London","prompt":"The diagram shows one simplified route through the rock cycle, linking igneous, sedimentary and metamorphic rocks.","topicWords":["rock","rocks","magma","igneous","sedimentary"],"stageModels":[{"label":"Magma","exact":["magma","molten"],"roots":["magma","molten"],"ref":"Molten rock exists below Earth's surface."},{"label":"Cooling","exact":["cools","cooling","crystallises"],"roots":["cools","cooling","crystallises"],"ref":"Magma cools and crystallises."},{"label":"Igneous rock","exact":["igneous","solid rock","igneous rock"],"roots":["igneous","solid"],"ref":"Solid igneous rock forms."},{"label":"Weathering","exact":["weathering","erosion","sediments"],"roots":["weathering","erosion","sediments"],"ref":"Weathering and erosion create sediments."},{"label":"Sedimentary rock","exact":["sedimentary","compaction","burial","sedimentary rock"],"roots":["sedimentary","compaction","burial"],"ref":"Deposition, burial and compaction form sedimentary rock."},{"label":"Heat and pressure","exact":["heat","pressure","buried","heat and pressure"],"roots":["heat","pressure","buried"],"ref":"Deep burial subjects rock to heat and pressure."},{"label":"Metamorphic rock","exact":["metamorphic","transforms","metamorphic rock"],"roots":["metamorphic","transforms"],"ref":"Rock transforms into metamorphic rock."},{"label":"Melting","exact":["melts","melting","magma"],"roots":["melts","melting","magma"],"ref":"Rock melts and becomes magma again."}],"analysis":{"type":["cyclical"],"stages":["8"],"first":["magma","molten"],"last":["melts","melting","magma"],"features":["magma","molten","cools","cooling","crystallises","igneous","solid rock","weathering","erosion","sediments","sedimentary","compaction","burial","heat","pressure","buried","metamorphic","transforms","melts","melting"],"body1":["magma","molten","cools","cooling","crystallises","igneous","solid rock","weathering","erosion","sediments"],"body2":["sedimentary","compaction","burial","heat","pressure","buried","metamorphic","transforms","melts","melting","magma"]},"intro":{"reference":"The diagram illustrates a simplified route through the rock cycle.","concepts":[["diagram","figure","process"],["illustrates","shows","depicts"],["rock","rocks","magma","igneous","sedimentary"],["stages","cycle","production","manufacture","illustrated"]],"minWords":8},"overview":{"reference":"Overall, the rock cycle is a repeating eight-stage sequence in this simplified diagram. Magma first cools into igneous rock, which can become sedimentary and metamorphic rock before melting back into magma. Heat, pressure and surface erosion drive these changes.","concepts":[["overall","in general"],["magma","molten","melts","melting","magma"],["stages","cycle","sequence","process"],["rock","rocks","magma","igneous","sedimentary"]],"minWords":35},"body1":{"reference":"The cycle starts with magma, or molten rock, beneath the Earth's surface. As the magma cools and crystallises, it solidifies to form igneous rock. When exposed at the surface, this rock undergoes weathering and erosion, producing small fragments known as sediments. These particles can then be transported and deposited in layers.","concepts":[["magma","molten"],["cools","cooling","crystallises"],["igneous","solid rock"],["weathering","erosion","sediments"]],"minWords":55,"sequence":["initially","at the beginning","first","then","after","next","once","subsequently"]},"body2":{"reference":"Over time, the deposited sediments are buried and compacted, eventually forming sedimentary rock. If this material is carried deeper underground, high temperatures and pressure transform it into metamorphic rock. Continued heating may then cause the rock to melt. The resulting magma can cool once more, restarting the cycle. The diagram presents one possible path rather than every geological transformation.","concepts":[["sedimentary","compaction","burial"],["heat","pressure","buried"],["metamorphic","transforms"],["melts","melting","magma"]],"minWords":55,"sequence":["finally","then","subsequently","after","once","eventually","next"]},"fullReference":"The diagram illustrates a simplified route through the rock cycle.\n\nOverall, the rock cycle is a repeating eight-stage sequence in this simplified diagram. Magma first cools into igneous rock, which can become sedimentary and metamorphic rock before melting back into magma. Heat, pressure and surface erosion drive these changes.\n\nThe cycle starts with magma, or molten rock, beneath the Earth's surface. As the magma cools and crystallises, it solidifies to form igneous rock. When exposed at the surface, this rock undergoes weathering and erosion, producing small fragments known as sediments. These particles can then be transported and deposited in layers.\n\nOver time, the deposited sediments are buried and compacted, eventually forming sedimentary rock. If this material is carried deeper underground, high temperatures and pressure transform it into metamorphic rock. Continued heating may then cause the rock to melt. The resulting magma can cool once more, restarting the cycle. The diagram presents one possible path rather than every geological transformation."};
window.PROCESS_TASKS["13"] = {"number":"13","title":"The Butterfly Life Cycle","icon":"🦋","image":"butterfly-life-cycle-approved.webp","type":"Life Cycle","stages":6,"category":"LifeCycle","description":"The diagram illustrates the life cycle of a butterfly through complete metamorphosis.","sourceUrl":"https://gardens.si.edu/learn/for-educators/cultivating-habitat/butterfly-gardening/about-butterflies/","sourceName":"Smithsonian Gardens","prompt":"The diagram illustrates the life cycle of a butterfly through complete metamorphosis.","topicWords":["butterfly","butterflies","caterpillar"],"stageModels":[{"label":"Eggs on leaves","exact":["eggs","leaves","host plant","eggs on leaves"],"roots":["eggs","leaves","host"],"ref":"An adult butterfly lays eggs on a suitable host plant."},{"label":"Hatching","exact":["hatch","caterpillars","larvae","hatching"],"roots":["hatch","caterpillars","larvae"],"ref":"Young caterpillars hatch from the eggs."},{"label":"Larval growth","exact":["feeds","moulting","growing","larval growth"],"roots":["feeds","moulting","growing"],"ref":"Caterpillars feed on leaves and grow, moulting as needed."},{"label":"Pupa","exact":["pupa","chrysalis"],"roots":["pupa","chrysalis"],"ref":"The mature larva forms a chrysalis, or pupa."},{"label":"Emergence","exact":["emerges","adult butterfly","emergence"],"roots":["emerges","adult"],"ref":"A new adult butterfly emerges from the chrysalis."},{"label":"Reproduction","exact":["mates","reproduces","lays eggs","reproduction"],"roots":["mates","reproduces","lays"],"ref":"The adult mates and lays eggs to begin another generation."}],"analysis":{"type":["life cycle"],"stages":["6"],"first":["eggs","leaves","host plant"],"last":["mates","reproduces","lays eggs"],"features":["eggs","leaves","host plant","hatch","caterpillars","larvae","feeds","moulting","growing","pupa","chrysalis","emerges","adult butterfly","mates","reproduces","lays eggs"],"body1":["eggs","leaves","host plant","hatch","caterpillars","larvae","feeds","moulting","growing"],"body2":["pupa","chrysalis","emerges","adult butterfly","mates","reproduces","lays eggs"]},"intro":{"reference":"The diagram illustrates the life cycle of a butterfly.","concepts":[["diagram","figure","process"],["illustrates","shows","depicts"],["butterfly","butterflies","caterpillar"],["stages","cycle","production","manufacture","illustrated"]],"minWords":8},"overview":{"reference":"Overall, the butterfly follows a cyclical six-stage life cycle involving complete metamorphosis. An egg develops into a feeding caterpillar, then into a pupa, before an adult butterfly emerges and lays eggs for the next generation.","concepts":[["overall","in general"],["eggs","leaves","host plant","mates","reproduces","lays eggs"],["stages","cycle","sequence","process"],["butterfly","butterflies","caterpillar"]],"minWords":35},"body1":{"reference":"Initially, a female butterfly lays eggs on the leaves of a suitable host plant. After development inside the eggs, small caterpillars hatch and begin feeding. During the larval stage, they eat plant material, increase in size and shed their outer skin several times as they grow.","concepts":[["eggs","leaves","host plant"],["hatch","caterpillars","larvae"],["feeds","moulting","growing"]],"minWords":55,"sequence":["initially","at the beginning","first","then","after","next","once","subsequently"]},"body2":{"reference":"When a caterpillar is fully developed, it forms a chrysalis, also called a pupa. Major changes occur inside this structure until an adult butterfly is ready to emerge. The new butterfly then expands its wings and becomes capable of flight. After reaching reproductive maturity, adults mate, and females lay further eggs on host plants, repeating the life cycle.","concepts":[["pupa","chrysalis"],["emerges","adult butterfly"],["mates","reproduces","lays eggs"]],"minWords":55,"sequence":["finally","then","subsequently","after","once","eventually","next"]},"fullReference":"The diagram illustrates the life cycle of a butterfly.\n\nOverall, the butterfly follows a cyclical six-stage life cycle involving complete metamorphosis. An egg develops into a feeding caterpillar, then into a pupa, before an adult butterfly emerges and lays eggs for the next generation.\n\nInitially, a female butterfly lays eggs on the leaves of a suitable host plant. After development inside the eggs, small caterpillars hatch and begin feeding. During the larval stage, they eat plant material, increase in size and shed their outer skin several times as they grow.\n\nWhen a caterpillar is fully developed, it forms a chrysalis, also called a pupa. Major changes occur inside this structure until an adult butterfly is ready to emerge. The new butterfly then expands its wings and becomes capable of flight. After reaching reproductive maturity, adults mate, and females lay further eggs on host plants, repeating the life cycle. This pattern continues in successive generations."};
